{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-108806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(5y-7x+2)^{5}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=5y-7x,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=5y-7x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{7}{5} & =(z+2)^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5(z+2)^{5}-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=5(z+2)^{5}-7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea86437202da364a04e8b6131fb6b1bb60009d4b2d2944956f7cba8d4c095841"} {"question" "q-108806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(z+2)^{5}-7"] ["math" "(z+5)^{2}"] ["math" "(z-2)^{5}+1"] ["math" "5(z+2)^{5}+1"] ["math" "5(z+2)^{5}-7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5(z+2)^{5}-7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-107797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+2y+5}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+2y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-107797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{z+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{z+5}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2{e}^{z+5}+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-107797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea86437202da364a04e8b6131fb6b1bb60009d4b2d2944956f7cba8d4c095841"} {"question" "q-107797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-107797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-{e}^{z+5}"] ["math" "2-5{e}^{z+5}"] ["math" "2{e}^{z+5}+1"] ["math" "2{e}^{z}-5"] ["math" "{e}^{z}+5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-107797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{e}^{z+5}+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={tan}^{2}⁡(3x-5y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=3x-5y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3x-5y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-5\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={tan}^{2}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea86437202da364a04e8b6131fb6b1bb60009d4b2d2944956f7cba8d4c095841"} {"question" "q-108802" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108802" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+{tan}^{2}⁡z"] ["math" "3+5\\operatorname{tan}⁡z"] ["math" "3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z"] ["math" "3-{tan}^{2}⁡(z-5)"] ["math" "5-3{tan}^{2}⁡z"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108802" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={cos}^{2}⁡(2x-y+1).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=2x-y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={cos}^{2}⁡(z+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-{cos}^{2}⁡(z+1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2-{cos}^{2}⁡(z+1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea86437202da364a04e8b6131fb6b1bb60009d4b2d2944956f7cba8d4c095841"} {"question" "q-108800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+{cos}^{2}⁡(2z)"] ["math" "2-{cos}^{2}⁡(z+1)"] ["math" "2-{cos}^{2}⁡z"] ["math" "2{cos}^{2}⁡(z+1)"] ["math" "{cos}^{2}⁡(2z+1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2-{cos}^{2}⁡(z+1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108821" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{(x+y)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108821" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108821" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea86437202da364a04e8b6131fb6b1bb60009d4b2d2944956f7cba8d4c095841"} {"question" "q-108821" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108821" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{z}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+1"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}-1"] ["math" "\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{e}^{2}}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108821" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{x+y}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}-1.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{x}{2}+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{\\sqrt{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}} & \\,⟹\\,v=2\\sqrt{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-4\\sqrt{z} & =x+K \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-2) & =x+K \\\\ \\sqrt{z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-2) & =\\frac{x}{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{K}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+y}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2) & =\\frac{x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea86437202da364a04e8b6131fb6b1bb60009d4b2d2944956f7cba8d4c095841"} {"question" "q-108856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2}{\\sqrt{x+y}}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+3"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{x+y}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{x+y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{x+y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{x+y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-107814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{y+3x+2}-3.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-107814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=y+3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3 & =\\sqrt{z+2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{z+2}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int (z+2)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2(z+2)^{1/2} & =x+C \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z+2} & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{y+3x+2} & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=2\\sqrt{y+3x+2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-107814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea86437202da364a04e8b6131fb6b1bb60009d4b2d2944956f7cba8d4c095841"} {"question" "q-107814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-107814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{y+3x+2}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{y+3x+2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{y+3x+2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{y+3x+2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{y+3x+2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-107814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{y+3x+2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-149721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-{e}^{y-3x}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-149721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=y-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3 & =3-{e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int {e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{-z} & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x-y}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={e}^{3x-y}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-149721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea86437202da364a04e8b6131fb6b1bb60009d4b2d2944956f7cba8d4c095841"} {"question" "q-149721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-149721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y-3x}"] ["math" "-{e}^{3x-y}"] ["math" "3{e}^{3x-y}"] ["math" "{e}^{3x-y}"] ["math" "{e}^{y-3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-149721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{3x-y}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{(x-2y)^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=C-x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}} & \\,⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{z}+\\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{z}-\\frac{1}{z} & =x+C \\\\ -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+1}{z} & =x+K \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+1}{z} & =C-x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+1}{x-2y}=C-x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+1}{x-2y}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea86437202da364a04e8b6131fb6b1bb60009d4b2d2944956f7cba8d4c095841"} {"question" "q-108855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+1}{x-2y}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)-2}{x-2y}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)}{x-2y}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+1}{x-2y}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{(2x+y)\\,{2}^{2x+y}}-2.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{1}{z\\,{2}^{z}}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{z\\,{2}^{z}}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={2}^{z} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z\\,{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z\\,{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x+C \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(z-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x+K \\\\ {2}^{z}(z-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=K\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea86437202da364a04e8b6131fb6b1bb60009d4b2d2944956f7cba8d4c095841"} {"question" "q-108858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{2}^{2x+y}}{2x+y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}"] ["math" "\\frac{{2}^{2x+y}}{2x+y}"] ["math" "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y)"] ["math" "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})"] ["math" "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "q-108858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbd082d8ee2fe734f945656ff739b18c491c3d55b38d77f8a2160d8f5b56bc92"} {"question" "e-9035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\ln(x + y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z= x + y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z'(x) = f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/example-9035.json" "file_sha256" "79923c43ab23d3b2e2029aaef25cf881ebee7fc6b8911651051986753945ba3d"} {"question" "e-9035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x + y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 1 + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\ln z+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z) = \\ln z + 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/example-9035.json" "file_sha256" "79923c43ab23d3b2e2029aaef25cf881ebee7fc6b8911651051986753945ba3d"} {"question" "e-9037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x + y.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/example-9037.json" "file_sha256" "0675985adcd555c48f4bbed0e84756cf9e0b031765fd061bafc036d84bf3fb6e"} {"question" "e-9037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 1 + \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = z+1.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{z+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{z+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|z+1| & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\ln{|x+y+1|}  = x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y) = \\ln{|x+y+1|}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14051698/example-9037.json" "file_sha256" "0675985adcd555c48f4bbed0e84756cf9e0b031765fd061bafc036d84bf3fb6e"}] "usage" {"e-9037" [] "e-9035" [] "q-107797" [] "q-107814" []} "basis" 1767 "retractions" [[17592186166993 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{x + 2y + 5}$.\nUsing a substitution of the form $z = x + 2y$, transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) = f(z)$. What is the function $f(z)$?"] [17592186166994 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sqrt{y + 3x + 2} - 3$.\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n$g(x, y) = x + C$,\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x, y)$?"] [17592186166630 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\ln(x + y)$.\nUsing a substitution of the form $z= x + y$, transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z'(x) = f(z)$. What is the function $f(z)$?"] [17592186166630 :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x + y\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - 1 &= \\ln z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form\n\n$$\nz'(x) = f(z)\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\ln z+1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(z) = \\ln z + 1\n$$"] [17592186166631 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x + y$.\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n$g(x,y) = x+C$,\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)$?"] [17592186166631 :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 + \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - 1 &= z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form\n\n$$\nz'(x) = f(z)\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = z+1\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{z + 1}\\frac{dz}{dx} &= 1 \\\\\n∫\\frac{1}{z + 1}dz &= ∫dx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid z + 1 \\mid &= x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\ln{\\mid x+y+1 \\mid} = x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = \\ln{\\mid x+y+1 \\mid}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186163377 17592186163378 17592186163379 17592186163380 17592186163381 17592186163383 17592186163384 17592186163385 17592186163386 17592186163387] "immutable_fields" [[17592186163376 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163376 :answer-field/id] [17592186163376 :answer-field/key] [17592186163376 :answer-field/type] [17592186163382 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163382 :answer-field/id] [17592186163382 :answer-field/key] [17592186163382 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
